html = '''<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Nike Inc. (NKE) — Historical Stock Performance Analysis (2000–2026)</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --navy: #1B2A4A;
    --blue: #1B4F72;
    --light-blue: #2980B9;
    --red: #C0392B;
    --green: #27AE60;
    --orange: #E67E22;
    --gray-50: #FAFAFA;
    --gray-100: #F5F5F5;
    --gray-200: #EEEEEE;
    --gray-300: #E0E0E0;
    --gray-500: #9E9E9E;
    --gray-700: #616161;
    --gray-900: #212121;
    --text: #333333;
    --text-light: #666666;
  }

  * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }

  body {
    font-family: 'Segoe UI', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;
    color: var(--text);
    background: #FFFFFF;
    line-height: 1.65;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  }

  .header {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--navy) 0%, var(--blue) 100%);
    color: white;
    padding: 48px 0 40px;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .header h1 { font-size: 2.1rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.5px; margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .header .subtitle { font-size: 1.05rem; opacity: 0.85; font-weight: 400; }
  .header .meta { margin-top: 18px; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.6; }

  .container { max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 32px; }

  /* KPI Dashboard */
  .kpi-strip {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
    gap: 0;
    margin: -28px auto 40px;
    max-width: 1100px;
    padding: 0 32px;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 10;
  }
  .kpi-card {
    background: #FFFFFF;
    padding: 22px 18px;
    text-align: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--gray-200);
    box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
  }
  .kpi-card:first-child { border-radius: 10px 0 0 10px; }
  .kpi-card:last-child { border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; }
  .kpi-card .label { font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1.2px; color: var(--gray-500); font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .kpi-card .value { font-size: 1.55rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
  .kpi-card .value.positive { color: var(--green); }
  .kpi-card .value.negative { color: var(--red); }
  .kpi-card .detail { font-size: 0.76rem; color: var(--gray-500); margin-top: 4px; }

  /* Sections */
  .section { margin-bottom: 52px; }
  .section-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    padding-bottom: 12px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--gray-200);
  }
  .section-number {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 32px; height: 32px;
    background: var(--navy);
    color: white;
    border-radius: 50%;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .section-header h2 { font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }

  p { margin-bottom: 14px; color: var(--text); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .text-muted { color: var(--text-light); }

  .chart-container {
    margin: 24px 0;
    background: var(--gray-50);
    border: 1px solid var(--gray-200);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 16px;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .chart-container img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 4px; }
  .chart-caption { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--gray-500); margin-top: 8px; font-style: italic; }

  /* Tables */
  .table-wrapper { overflow-x: auto; margin: 20px 0; }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }
  thead th {
    background: var(--navy);
    color: white;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
  }
  tbody td {
    padding: 9px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--gray-200);
  }
  tbody tr:nth-child(even) { background: var(--gray-50); }
  tbody tr:hover { background: #EBF5FB; }
  .pos { color: var(--green); font-weight: 600; }
  .neg { color: var(--red); font-weight: 600; }

  /* Callout boxes */
  .callout {
    background: #EBF5FB;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--light-blue);
    padding: 16px 20px;
    margin: 20px 0;
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .callout.warning {
    background: #FDEDEC;
    border-left-color: var(--red);
  }
  .callout strong { color: var(--navy); }

  /* Two-column grid */
  .two-col {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 24px;
    margin: 20px 0;
  }
  .col-card {
    background: var(--gray-50);
    border: 1px solid var(--gray-200);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }
  .col-card h4 { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--navy); margin-bottom: 10px; font-weight: 700; }
  .col-card ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .col-card li { margin-bottom: 5px; }

  .footer {
    margin-top: 60px;
    padding: 28px 0;
    background: var(--gray-100);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--gray-300);
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--gray-500);
  }

  /* Decade summary boxes */
  .decade-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 24px 0;
  }
  .decade-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--gray-200);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
    text-align: center;
    background: #fff;
  }
  .decade-card h4 { font-size: 1rem; color: var(--navy); margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .decade-card .stat { font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 700; }
  .decade-card .sublabel { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--gray-500); margin-top: 2px; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .kpi-strip { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
    .kpi-card:first-child { border-radius: 10px 0 0 0; }
    .kpi-card:last-child { border-radius: 0 0 10px 0; }
    .two-col, .decade-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .kpi-strip { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
    .container { padding: 0 16px; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<!-- ======================== HEADER ======================== -->
<div class="header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Nike Inc. (NKE) &mdash; Historical Stock Performance Analysis</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">A 26-Year Retrospective for Long-Term Investors &nbsp;|&nbsp; January 2000 &ndash; January 2026</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Source: Historical daily adjusted prices &amp; volume &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; 6,559 trading days analyzed</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ======================== KPI DASHBOARD ======================== -->
<div class="kpi-strip">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Cumulative Return</div>
    <div class="value positive">+1,282%</div>
    <div class="detail">$4.47 &rarr; $61.81</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">CAGR</div>
    <div class="value positive">10.60%</div>
    <div class="detail">26.1 years compounded</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Maximum Drawdown</div>
    <div class="value negative">&minus;68.5%</div>
    <div class="detail">Nov 2021 &rarr; Apr 2025</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Annualized Volatility</div>
    <div class="value">31.2%</div>
    <div class="detail">Based on daily returns</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="label">Avg Daily Volume</div>
    <div class="value">10.7M</div>
    <div class="detail">Shares per day</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- ======================== SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ======================== -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">1</span>
    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
  </div>

  <p>Over the 26-year period from January 3, 2000 to January 29, 2026, Nike Inc. (NKE) delivered a <strong>cumulative return of +1,282%</strong>, transforming every $1,000 invested into approximately $13,820. This translates to a <strong>compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of 10.60%</strong>, comfortably outpacing the long-run average return of the S&P 500.</p>

  <p>However, the path was far from smooth. Nike&rsquo;s stock exhibited an <strong>annualized volatility of 31.2%</strong> and endured a <strong>maximum drawdown of &minus;68.5%</strong> from its November 2021 all-time high of $166.25 to $52.31 in April 2025 &mdash; the deepest trough in the dataset. This underscores that even among elite compounders, investors must be prepared for multi-year periods of severe capital impairment.</p>

  <p>The stock&rsquo;s journey divides into three distinct macro-phases: a <strong>steady recovery and growth era (2000&ndash;2009)</strong> following the dot-com and early-2000s downturn, a <strong>spectacular bull run (2010&ndash;2021)</strong> driven by DTC transformation and global brand power, and a <strong>sharp retracement (2022&ndash;2026)</strong> that erased over two-thirds of the peak valuation. At $61.81, the stock ended January 2026 near levels last seen in early 2018.</p>
</div>

<!-- ======================== SECTION 2: PRICE TREND ANALYSIS ======================== -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">2</span>
    <h2>Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</h2>
  </div>

  <p>The chart below presents Nike&rsquo;s full price history with 50-day and 200-day simple moving averages, shaded regions for notable crisis periods, and markers for the all-time high and low.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,''' + charts['price_trend'] + '''" alt="NKE Closing Price with Moving Averages (2000-2026)">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 1: NKE daily adjusted closing price with 50-day and 200-day SMAs. Shaded areas indicate crisis / bear-market periods.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,''' + charts['price_log'] + '''" alt="NKE Log-Scale Price (2000-2026)">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 2: Log-scale view reveals the compounding growth rate and the magnitude of the post-2021 decline relative to the long-term trend.</div>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Phase 1 — Base Formation &amp; Recovery (2000&ndash;2003):</strong> Nike&rsquo;s adjusted close began at $4.47 in January 2000, immediately fell to its all-time low of $2.47 by February 2000 (a &minus;45% decline in under two months driven by earnings disappointments and broader dot-com contagion), then spent roughly three years building a base before breaking out above $6 in late 2003 (+49.3% for the year).</p>

  <p><strong>Phase 2 — Steady Appreciation (2004&ndash;2007):</strong> Nike posted consistent gains, rising from ~$6.50 to ~$12.60. The stock gained +34.9% in 2004 and +33.1% in 2007, establishing a pattern of steady mid-teens to mid-thirties annual returns. The 200-day SMA trended upward nearly uninterrupted.</p>

  <p><strong>Phase 3 — Global Financial Crisis (2008&ndash;2009):</strong> The GFC clipped Nike by &minus;18.1% in 2008, but this was a relatively mild drawdown compared to the broader market. The recovery was swift: +26.9% in 2009, with the stock closing above its pre-crisis high by late that year. This resilience was an early signal of Nike&rsquo;s defensive quality characteristics.</p>

  <p><strong>Phase 4 — The Great Bull Run (2010&ndash;2021):</strong> This twelve-year stretch was extraordinary. Nike rose from $13.27 to an all-time high of $166.25 in November 2021 &mdash; a <strong>~12.5x appreciation</strong>. Key catalysts included consistent revenue growth, margin expansion via the direct-to-consumer pivot, international expansion (particularly China), and multiple expansion as the market rewarded Nike&rsquo;s premium brand positioning. Standout years included 2013 (+53.8%), 2019 (+38.2%), and 2020 (+39.7%). The COVID-19 crash in March 2020 caused a brief &minus;36% intraday decline but was fully recovered within months, fueled by e-commerce acceleration.</p>

  <p><strong>Phase 5 — Post-Peak Decline (2022&ndash;2026):</strong> From the November 2021 peak at $166.25, the stock entered a persistent downtrend. The decline accelerated through multiple catalysts: inventory overhang in 2022 (&minus;28.2%), competitive headwinds and China uncertainty in 2023 (&minus;7.4%), and strategic missteps in 2024 (&minus;27.8%). By April 2025, the stock touched $52.31 — a &minus;68.5% drawdown from peak, the deepest in the dataset. At $61.81 as of late January 2026, recovery remains tentative.</p>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Key Observation:</strong> The &minus;68.5% maximum drawdown (Nov 2021 to Apr 2025) is the most severe decline in the dataset, surpassing even the early-2000 crash. At 3.4 years in duration, it also represents the longest sustained decline, testing the conviction of long-term holders.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ======================== SECTION 3: DRAWDOWN AND VOLATILITY ======================== -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">3</span>
    <h2>Volatility and Risk Assessment</h2>
  </div>

  <p>Volatility is the price of admission for equity returns. Nike&rsquo;s annualized volatility of 31.2% — calculated from daily log returns over 6,559 trading days — positions it as moderately volatile for a large-cap consumer brand. The chart below shows how this risk materialized as drawdowns from peak value.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,''' + charts['drawdown'] + '''" alt="NKE Drawdown Chart">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 3: Drawdown from prior peak. The post-2021 drawdown of &minus;68.5% dwarfs all prior episodes.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,''' + charts['volatility'] + '''" alt="NKE Rolling Volatility">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 4: 30-day rolling annualized volatility. Peaks align with the 2008 GFC (~95%) and COVID-19 crash (~92%).</div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,''' + charts['daily_range'] + '''" alt="NKE Daily Range">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 5: 60-day rolling average of daily price range as a percentage of closing price — a proxy for intraday volatility.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,''' + charts['return_dist'] + '''" alt="NKE Daily Return Distribution">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 6: Distribution of daily returns. The distribution exhibits mild negative skew and fat tails typical of equity returns.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="col-card">
      <h4>Major Drawdown Episodes</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Feb 2000:</strong> &minus;45% in 7 weeks (earnings miss + dot-com)</li>
        <li><strong>2008 GFC:</strong> &minus;40% peak-to-trough; recovered by mid-2009</li>
        <li><strong>COVID-19 (Mar 2020):</strong> ~&minus;36% intraday; full recovery in 5 months</li>
        <li><strong>Nov 2021 &ndash; Apr 2025:</strong> &minus;68.5% over 3.4 years &mdash; the deepest and longest</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="col-card">
      <h4>Volatility Regime Analysis</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Highest 30d vol:</strong> ~95% annualized (Nov 2008, GFC)</li>
        <li><strong>Second highest:</strong> ~92% annualized (Apr 2020, COVID)</li>
        <li><strong>Typical calm regime:</strong> 15&ndash;25% annualized</li>
        <li><strong>Daily range in crises:</strong> 5&ndash;8% vs normal 1.5&ndash;2.5%</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The volatility time series reveals distinct regimes. The 2000s were structurally more volatile than the 2010s, likely reflecting Nike&rsquo;s smaller market cap and less institutional ownership in the earlier period. The 2020s have seen volatility re-elevate, driven by both the COVID shock and the sustained post-2021 decline.</p>
</div>

<!-- ======================== SECTION 4: VOLUME ANALYSIS ======================== -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">4</span>
    <h2>Trading Volume Patterns</h2>
  </div>

  <p>Volume tells us <em>how much conviction</em> accompanied price movements. The chart below overlays price and monthly average volume, with green/red bars indicating months with net positive/negative returns.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,''' + charts['price_volume'] + '''" alt="NKE Price and Volume">
    <div class="chart-caption">Figure 7: NKE price (top) and monthly average volume (bottom). Green = net positive month, Red = net negative month.</div>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Secular volume decline (2000s &rarr; 2010s):</strong> Average daily volume fell from ~12.5M shares in the 2000s to ~9.0M in the 2010s, even as the stock price rose dramatically. This likely reflects the shift toward institutional buy-and-hold strategies, ETF inclusion, and reduced speculative turnover as Nike became a widely-held blue chip.</p>

  <p><strong>Volume resurgence (2024&ndash;2026):</strong> Average daily volume surged to ~11.6M in 2024 and ~16.7M in 2025 — the highest levels since the mid-2000s. This aligns with the accelerating price decline, suggesting institutional portfolio rebalancing and active selling pressure.</p>

  <div class="table-wrapper">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Date</th>
        <th>Volume</th>
        <th>Close</th>
        <th>Daily Return</th>
        <th>Context</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Jun 28, 2024</td><td>130.0M</td><td>$72.94</td><td class="neg">&minus;20.0%</td><td>Earnings shock — largest single-day volume in dataset</td></tr>
